From: PS4 port
Subject: [PATCH] orbis: turn the recompiler on, without the fastmem it cannot have

melondsds shipped with -DENABLE_JIT=OFF because ARMJIT_Memory.cpp would not compile:

    ARMJIT_Memory.cpp:214:34: error: no member named 'CONTEXT_PC' in '__ucontext'
    ARMJIT_Memory.cpp:218:18: error: no member named 'CONTEXT_PC' in '__ucontext'

⚠ THAT IS THE WHOLE OF IT. With ENABLE_JIT=ON and nothing else changed, ONE object out of the
build fails - ARMJIT_Memory.cpp - and it fails on those two lines. The entire ARM-to-x64
recompiler compiles: ARMJIT.cpp, ARMJIT_Global.cpp, every file under ARMJIT_x64/, dolphin's
x64Emitter and x64CPUDetect, and the ARMJIT_Linkage.S assembly. A DS emulator on a pure
interpreter is a different machine from one with a recompiler, so this is worth the patch.

⚠ AND CONTEXT_PC CANNOT BE GIVEN AN ARM HERE. melonDS names a per-OS member of the signal
context; the file has arms for Windows, Linux, Apple, FreeBSD and NetBSD, and none matches
because this SDK's libc++ does `#undef __FreeBSD__` and this is a C++ translation unit. Supplying
one would be a guess: the SDK ships musl's ucontext (uc_mcontext.gregs[]) while the kernel passes
FreeBSD's, and orbis-compat's crash handler has already dumped thirty-two words of this exact
pointer on hardware and recorded that NONE of them was a text address. The offset of rip on this
console is not known, and this handler WRITES the field it names. Same conclusion as
flycast/0001-orbis-no-usable-segfault-context.patch and play/0004.

So fastmem goes off and the recompiler stays on - which melonDS supports directly: FastMemory is
a runtime flag and ARMJIT_Memory::IsFastMemSupported() is the choke point every path routes
through (ARMJIT.cpp:497, `args.FastMemory = args.FastMemory && IsFastMemSupported()`).

⚠ AND THE PAGE-SIZE TEST WOULD HAVE SAID YES, WHICH IS THE TRAP. IsFastMemSupported() accepts
`PageSize == RegularPageSize || PageSize == LargePageSize`, and ARMJIT_Memory.h:53 defines
LargePageSize as 0x4000 - exactly this console's 16 KiB granule. Left alone it would enable
fastmem, install the handler, and rewrite a program counter read from an offset nobody has
measured. The check is short-circuited explicitly rather than left to the arithmetic.

⚠ AND THE HANDLER IS NOT INSTALLED AT ALL, which matters beyond fastmem. RegisterFaultHandler
takes SIGSEGV process-wide, which would take it from orbis-compat's crash reporter - the only
thing on this console that turns a fault into a diagnosis. And the handler's first act is to
dereference NDS::Current, a thread_local that is null on every thread not running the emulator,
so any unrelated fault would become a null dereference inside the SIGSEGV handler.

⚠ THE CODE BUFFER NEEDED NO REDESIGN, WHICH IS THE PLEASANT SURPRISE. melonDS already allocates
its JIT memory as `u8 CodeMemory[...]` in .bss, already aligns it to 16 KiB, and already promotes
it in place with one mprotect - the exact shape flycast had to be rewritten into
(flycast/0002, /0003). Being in .bss means it is inside the module image, so the recompiler's
rel32 calls into its own code reach by construction and the `offset is too big` failure flycast
hit with an mmap'd cache cannot happen. Only the promotion call is swapped: mprotect is not
interposed on this platform, while ps4/orbis_exec_mem.c rounds to the 16 KiB granule, uses
sceKernelMprotect, proves the result by executing six bytes out of the block, and reports
granted-or-refused per range in the console log.

⚠ WHAT THE MODULE NEEDS AT LINK TIME: orbis_exec_mem_promote, from ps4/orbis_exec_mem.c in
liborbis-core-support.a. It is an archive member, so it is pulled only because this reference
exists - and that archive is rebuilt only when it is ABSENT. Delete it before relinking.

⚠ AND A SECOND WALL BEHIND THE FIRST, FOUND ON HARDWARE: THE 8 GiB RESERVATION.

With fastmem off and the recompiler on, the console granted the 128 MiB code buffer - the largest
promotion this port has ever asked for - and then died at content load:

    fatal: signal 11 - SIGSEGV, si_code 2 (SEGV_ACCERR), addr 0000000001017fff
    fatal: rip 00000008008eea00  trapno 12  err 7

rip is memset+0x50 in this module (the movdqu store loop, so rdi is the destination), err 7 is
present + write + user, and 0x01017fff is inside the FRONTEND's r-x text - a write to somebody
else's code, which is why it is ACCERR and not an unmapped address.

The destination is arithmetic, not a guess. ARMJIT_Memory's constructor opens with

    MemoryBase = (u8*)mmap(nullptr, VirtmemAreaSize, PROT_NONE, MAP_ANON | MAP_PRIVATE, -1, 0);

and never asks whether it worked - nor does the MAP_FIXED mmap after it. VirtmemAreaSize is
AddrSpaceSize*2 + MemoryTotalSize = 8.0 GiB, because the two fastmem mirrors are 4 GiB each. This
console will not reserve that. ⚠ AND MAP_FAILED IS (void*)-1 HERE, NOT NULL, so the failure does
not read as a null pointer to anything downstream: every block pointer becomes `offset - 1`, a
SMALL address that lands wherever else the process has mapped things. melonDS then clears the
blocks, and the first one through MemoryBase reports it:

    MemBlockDTCMOffset          = 0x01018000
    (u8*)-1 + 0x01018000        = 0x01017fff        <- the fault address, to the byte

⚠ AND THE FIX IS TO STOP ASKING, NOT TO CHECK AND BAIL. The 8 GiB is ENTIRELY for the mirrors;
the emulator needs MemoryTotalSize - about 17 MiB - of ordinary writable memory. Since
IsFastMemSupported() is already false here, the reservation, the shm_open, the ftruncate and the
MAP_FIXED mapping are all dead weight. melonDS already ships the allocation for exactly this
situation one branch up - `MemoryBase = new u8[MemoryTotalSize]`, its old-Windows path - so this
takes that, nulls the two mirror pointers, and pairs the destructor with delete[].

⚠ AND THE MELONDS SOURCES ARE FETCHED, WHICH IS WHY THIS PATCH LOOKS THE WAY IT DOES.
build/_deps/melonds-src does not exist when the port applies its patches, so nothing this
repository's patch tree can do reaches it. FetchContent's own PATCH_COMMAND is the mechanism for
that, and it runs once, at populate. So this patch adds the melonDS diff as a file in THIS
repository and teaches fetch_dependency() to pass it through. Any other FetchContent-based core
that needs a dependency fixed will need the same shape.

diff --git a/cmake/FetchDependencies.cmake b/cmake/FetchDependencies.cmake
index 7265cb2..f11f10e 100644
--- a/cmake/FetchDependencies.cmake
+++ b/cmake/FetchDependencies.cmake
@@ -23,9 +23,21 @@ macro(define_git_dependency_vars name default_url default_tag)
     endif ()
 endmacro()
 
+# ⚠ ARGN IS AN OPTIONAL PATCH FILE, AND IT IS THE ONLY WAY TO REACH A FETCHED SOURCE.
+# The PS4 port applies its per-core patches to THIS repository's checkout before CMake runs, so
+# nothing it can do reaches build/_deps - those trees do not exist yet at that point. A dependency
+# that needs a platform fix therefore has to be patched by the populate step itself, which is what
+# FetchContent's PATCH_COMMAND is for. It runs once, when the source is first checked out.
 function(fetch_dependency name default_url default_tag)
     define_git_dependency_vars(${name} ${default_url} ${default_tag})
 
+    set(patch_args "")
+    if (ARGN AND NOT FETCHCONTENT_SOURCE_DIR_${VAR_NAME})
+        list(GET ARGN 0 patch_file)
+        message(STATUS "Patching ${name} with ${patch_file}")
+        set(patch_args PATCH_COMMAND "${GIT_EXECUTABLE}" apply --ignore-whitespace "${patch_file}")
+    endif()
+
     if (FETCHCONTENT_SOURCE_DIR_${VAR_NAME})
         message(STATUS "Using ${name}: ${FETCHCONTENT_SOURCE_DIR_${VAR_NAME}} (local)")
     else()
@@ -36,12 +48,14 @@ function(fetch_dependency name default_url default_tag)
         ${name}
         GIT_REPOSITORY "${${VAR_NAME}_REPOSITORY_URL}"
         GIT_TAG "${${VAR_NAME}_REPOSITORY_TAG}"
+        ${patch_args}
     )
 
     FetchContent_GetProperties(${name})
 endfunction()
 
-fetch_dependency(melonDS "https://github.com/melonDS-emu/melonDS" "7117178")
+fetch_dependency(melonDS "https://github.com/melonDS-emu/melonDS" "7117178"
+        "${CMAKE_SOURCE_DIR}/ps4-patches/melonds-orbis-jit-no-fastmem.patch")
 fetch_dependency(libretro-common "https://github.com/libretro/libretro-common" "ad9124f")
 fetch_dependency("embed-binaries" "https://github.com/andoalon/embed-binaries" "078b62b")
 fetch_dependency(glm "https://github.com/g-truc/glm" "e7970a8")
diff --git a/ps4-patches/melonds-orbis-jit-no-fastmem.patch b/ps4-patches/melonds-orbis-jit-no-fastmem.patch
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..a1e170a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/ps4-patches/melonds-orbis-jit-no-fastmem.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,169 @@
+diff --git a/src/ARMJIT_Global.cpp b/src/ARMJIT_Global.cpp
+index c9432cbf..05b2cef2 100644
+--- a/src/ARMJIT_Global.cpp
++++ b/src/ARMJIT_Global.cpp
+@@ -1,6 +1,13 @@
+ #include "ARMJIT_Global.h"
+ #include "ARMJIT_Memory.h"
+ 
++#ifdef __ORBIS__
++/* Defined in RetroArch/ps4/orbis_exec_mem.c, which the port's build harness links into every
++ * core through liborbis-core-support.a. Declared rather than included: that file ships no
++ * header a fetched dependency could reach. */
++extern "C" int orbis_exec_mem_promote(void *addr, size_t len);
++#endif
++
+ #ifdef _WIN32
+ #include <windows.h>
+ #else
+@@ -102,6 +109,22 @@ void Init()
+             VirtualProtect(GetAlignedCodeMemoryStart(), CodeMemoryAlignedSize, PAGE_EXECUTE_READWRITE, &dummy);
+         #elif defined(APPLE_AARCH64)
+             // Apple aarch64 always uses dynamic allocation
++        #elif defined(__ORBIS__)
++            // ⚠ sceKernelMprotect, NOT mprotect, AND IT IS NOT INTERCHANGEABLE HERE. This kernel
++            // refuses PROT_EXEC at map time and grants it afterwards, but only in 16 KiB units and
++            // only through Sony's own call; RetroArch/ps4/orbis_exec_mem.c does that, rounds the
++            // range outwards to the granule, and then PROVES the promotion by writing six bytes of
++            // x86-64 into the block and calling them. It also reports granted-or-refused for each
++            // range in the console log, which is the only way to tell "this console will not do
++            // that" from "we asked wrong".
++            //
++            // CodeMemory is a .bss array, so it is inside the module image and the recompiler's
++            // rel32 calls into its own code reach by construction - the failure flycast hit when
++            // its cache was mmap'd two gigabytes away cannot happen here.
++            // The result is deliberately not logged here: orbis_exec_mem.c prints its own
++            // granted-or-refused line for every range it is asked about, with the Sony error
++            // code spelled out, which is strictly more than this call site could say.
++            (void)orbis_exec_mem_promote(GetAlignedCodeMemoryStart(), CodeMemoryAlignedSize);
+         #else
+             mprotect(GetAlignedCodeMemoryStart(), CodeMemoryAlignedSize, PROT_EXEC | PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE);
+         #endif
+diff --git a/src/ARMJIT_Memory.cpp b/src/ARMJIT_Memory.cpp
+index 748b6710..cc2301dd 100644
+--- a/src/ARMJIT_Memory.cpp
++++ b/src/ARMJIT_Memory.cpp
+@@ -187,6 +187,23 @@ LONG ARMJIT_Memory::ExceptionHandler(EXCEPTION_POINTERS* exceptionInfo)
+     return EXCEPTION_CONTINUE_SEARCH;
+ }
+ 
++#elif defined(__ORBIS__)
++
++// ⚠ THE PLAYSTATION 4 CANNOT NAME THE FAULTING INSTRUCTION, so this handler cannot exist.
++// CONTEXT_PC above has no arm for this platform and cannot be given one: the SDK ships musl's
++// ucontext (uc_mcontext.gregs[]) while the kernel passes FreeBSD's, and orbis-compat's crash
++// handler has already dumped thirty-two words of this exact pointer on hardware and recorded
++// that NONE of them was a text address - the offset of rip is not something to look up here,
++// it is something still to be measured. Naming a field would be a guess dressed up as a
++// register, and this handler WRITES the one it names.
++// The body is empty rather than the file's, and RegisterFaultHandler below does not install
++// it, so nothing reads or writes a context. IsFastMemSupported() returns false to match:
++// fastmem is the only feature that needs this, and the JIT itself does not.
++void ARMJIT_Memory::SigsegvHandler(int sig, siginfo_t* info, void* rawContext)
++{
++    (void)sig; (void)info; (void)rawContext;
++}
++
+ #else
+ 
+ static struct sigaction OldSaSegv;
+@@ -739,7 +756,12 @@ u32 ARMJIT_Memory::PageShift = 0;
+ 
+ bool ARMJIT_Memory::IsFastMemSupported()
+ {
+-#ifdef __APPLE__
++// ⚠ __ORBIS__: not "unsupported page size" - unsupported fault handler. The test below would
++// SAY YES here, because this console's page granule is 16 KiB and that is exactly
++// LargePageSize. Fastmem needs a SIGSEGV handler that rewrites the faulting instruction
++// pointer, and this kernel does not hand one over in any layout we can name - see
++// SigsegvHandler above.
++#if defined(__APPLE__) || defined(__ORBIS__)
+     return false;
+ #else
+     static bool initialised = false;
+@@ -779,6 +801,13 @@ void ARMJIT_Memory::RegisterFaultHandler()
+     {
+         Log(LogLevel::Error, "Could not load new Windows virtual memory functions, fast memory is disabled.\n");
+     }
++#elif defined(__ORBIS__)
++    // ⚠ NOT INSTALLED, AND NOT ONLY BECAUSE IT WOULD BE USELESS. This is a process-wide
++    // handler: taking SIGSEGV here would take it away from orbis-compat's crash reporter, which
++    // is the only thing on this console that turns a fault into a diagnosis. Worse, the handler's
++    // first act is to dereference NDS::Current - a thread_local that is null on every thread that
++    // is not running the emulator - so a fault anywhere else in the process would become a null
++    // dereference INSIDE the SIGSEGV handler. With fastmem off there is nothing for it to do.
+ #else
+     struct sigaction sa;
+     sa.sa_handler = nullptr;
+@@ -806,6 +835,8 @@ void ARMJIT_Memory::UnregisterFaultHandler()
+         FreeLibrary(KernelBaseDll);
+         KernelBaseDll = nullptr;
+     }
++#elif defined(__ORBIS__)
++    // Nothing was installed; see RegisterFaultHandler.
+ #else
+     sigaction(SIGSEGV, &OldSaSegv, nullptr);
+ #ifdef __APPLE__
+@@ -879,6 +910,34 @@ ARMJIT_Memory::ARMJIT_Memory(melonDS::NDS& nds) : NDS(nds)
+         // old Windows version
+         MemoryBase = new u8[MemoryTotalSize];
+     }
++#elif defined(__ORBIS__)
++    // ⚠ NO 8 GiB RESERVATION HERE, AND NOTHING BELOW IT EITHER. The POSIX arm reserves
++    // VirtmemAreaSize = AddrSpaceSize*2 + MemoryTotalSize = 8.0 GiB of PROT_NONE address space so
++    // that the two 4 GiB fastmem mirrors have somewhere to live. This console will not do that,
++    // and melonDS never asks whether it worked:
++    //
++    //     MemoryBase = (u8*)mmap(nullptr, VirtmemAreaSize, PROT_NONE, ...);   // unchecked
++    //
++    // MAP_FAILED is (void*)-1 here, not NULL, so a failed reservation does not look like a null
++    // pointer to anything downstream - it makes every block pointer `offset - 1`, a SMALL address
++    // that lands in whatever else the process has mapped down there. Measured on hardware, exactly:
++    //
++    //     fatal: signal 11 - SIGSEGV, si_code 2 (SEGV_ACCERR), addr 0000000001017fff
++    //     fatal: rip 00000008008eea00        <- memset+0x50, the movdqu store loop
++    //
++    //     MemBlockDTCMOffset                        = 0x01018000
++    //     (u8*)-1 + 0x01018000                      = 0x01017fff   <- to the byte
++    //
++    // and 0x01017fff is inside the FRONTEND's r-x text mapping, which is why it faults as a WRITE
++    // TO A PRESENT PAGE (err 7) rather than as an unmapped address. The first block melonDS clears
++    // through MemoryBase is what reports it; DTCM happens to be that block.
++    //
++    // ⚠ AND WITH FASTMEM OFF THE RESERVATION IS NOT WANTED IN THE FIRST PLACE. The 8 GiB is
++    // ENTIRELY for the mirrors; the emulator itself needs MemoryTotalSize - about 17 MiB - of
++    // ordinary writable memory. IsFastMemSupported() already returns false on this platform (see
++    // above), so this takes the allocation melonDS already ships for the same situation on old
++    // Windows, one branch up.
++    MemoryBase = new u8[MemoryTotalSize];
+ #else
+     MemoryBase = (u8*)mmap(nullptr, VirtmemAreaSize, PROT_NONE, MAP_ANON | MAP_PRIVATE, -1, 0);
+ 
+@@ -917,6 +976,13 @@ ARMJIT_Memory::ARMJIT_Memory(melonDS::NDS& nds) : NDS(nds)
+ #endif
+     FastMem9Start = MemoryBase+MemoryTotalSize;
+     FastMem7Start = static_cast<u8*>(FastMem9Start)+AddrSpaceSize;
++#ifdef __ORBIS__
++    // Nothing was reserved, so these address two mirrors that do not exist. Null rather than
++    // one-past-a-17-MiB-heap-block: IsFastMemSupported() is false so nothing should read them,
++    // and if that ever stops being true the null is at least a stated absence.
++    FastMem9Start = nullptr;
++    FastMem7Start = nullptr;
++#endif
+ }
+ 
+ ARMJIT_Memory::~ARMJIT_Memory() noexcept
+@@ -963,6 +1029,13 @@ ARMJIT_Memory::~ARMJIT_Memory() noexcept
+     {
+         delete[] MemoryBase;
+     }
++#elif defined(__ORBIS__)
++    // Paired with `new u8[MemoryTotalSize]` above. No munmap: nothing was mapped. No MemoryFile
++    // either - the shm_open/ftruncate dance below is only there to back the fastmem mirrors.
++    delete[] MemoryBase;
++    MemoryBase = nullptr;
++    FastMem9Start = nullptr;
++    FastMem7Start = nullptr;
+ #else
+     if (MemoryBase)
+     {
